# White-paper content, part B: Results
# Every number below traces to a query run against srs_tank_waste.db on 2026-10-16.

RESULTS = [
    {"t": "h1", "text": "2. Results"},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "2.1 Site-level totals"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Table 1 summarizes the two sites' remaining inventories on their own published bases. SRS reports 33.2 Mgal and 191 MCi as of September 30, 2025, from the WCS Curie and Volume Inventory Report transmitted with Liquid Waste System Plan Revision 24. Hanford's BBI, summed over the latest per-tank snapshots (effective dates between 2015 and 2026, dominated by 2022 through 2026), gives 53.0 Mgal of physical waste (excluding drained-void and retained-gas volume buckets) and 122.5 MCi decayed predominantly to January 1, 2022. On these numbers SRS holds 63 percent of Hanford's volume but 156 percent of its activity: an activity density of 5.8 MCi/Mgal versus 2.3 at Hanford."},
    {"t": "tbl1"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The activity-density ratio is not an artifact of the SRS closure basis. The 191 MCi figure is a measured, current farm-level total, and the closure-residual dataset (5.80 MCi total across 51 tanks) is more than an order of magnitude below it precisely because closure residuals assume waste removal succeeds. The density contrast therefore compares current SRS waste against current Hanford waste on their respective published bases. Its cause is chemical: SRS evaporated its supernate to saltcake under a chemistry that kept cesium mobile in concentrated nitrate-nitrite brine, so salt waste at SRS holds 45 percent of site activity in 91 percent of site volume. Hanford's older waste generated saltcake with the bulk of strontium-90 and a substantial fraction of cesium in sludge and solids, as shown in the phase split below."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "2.2 Phase splits"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Figure 1 shows the two sites' volume and activity by phase. SRS phase data come from the same WCS report: sludge 2.8 Mgal holding 106 MCi, supernate 16.4 Mgal holding 75 MCi, and saltcake 10.0 Mgal holding 10 MCi. Hanford's BBI volumes by phase (physical waste only) are supernatant 22.3 Mgal, saltcake solids plus interstitial liquid 21.3 Mgal, and sludge solids plus interstitial liquid 10.4 Mgal; the activity split is sludge 67.6 MCi, supernatant 30.2 MCi, and saltcake 24.7 MCi. SRS sludge is 8 percent of site volume but 55 percent of site activity; Hanford sludge is 20 percent of volume and 55 percent of activity. The two sites agree that sludge is the curie-dominant phase per gallon, but SRS carries nearly half its activity in mobile liquid phases (supernate plus saltcake interstitial), while Hanford keeps only a quarter of activity in its liquid phases."},
    {"t": "fig", "file": "fig2_phase_split.png", "caption": "Figure 1. Remaining waste volume by phase at each site, on each site's own published basis. SRS values are WCS farm totals as of 2025-09-30; Hanford values are the sum of latest per-tank BBI volumes, excluding drained-void and retained-gas buckets."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "A second-order difference is within-phase consistency. Hanford's DSTs hold mostly supernatant (27.5 Mgal across 28 tanks), and SSTs hold mostly saltcake and sludge (25.5 Mgal across 149 tanks), with activity split 69.3 MCi DST / 53.2 MCi SST. SRS has no DST/SST distinction in the same sense: all 51 tanks are single-wall but every tank sits inside a secondary containment arrangement (panned annulus for older types, double containment for Type III/IV). Hanford's 29 tanks under 50 kL of waste are effectively empty, awaiting closure or use as spare capacity; SRS's closed tanks hold assigned residuals only."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "2.3 Radionuclide composition"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Figure 2 compares the 12 highest-activity nuclides at each site on a log scale, and Table 2 gives the corresponding values. Because the sites report a different analyte set (SRS closure residuals list 54 nuclides; Hanford's BBI lists 50 nuclides plus aggregates like TotalAlpha), the comparison uses site totals per nuclide rather than per-tank values. Two families dominate both sites: strontium-90/yttrium-90 and cesium-137/barium-137m, each counted as a parent-plus-short-lived-daughter pair in secular equilibrium. Together the two pairs account for 97 percent of SRS current activity (Sr-90+Y-90 49 percent, Cs-137+Ba-137m 48 percent per the LWSP phase data) and 95 percent of Hanford BBI activity (Sr+Y 53 percent, Cs+Ba 42 percent)."},
    {"t": "fig", "file": "fig3_nuclides.png", "caption": "Figure 2. Site-total activity for the top 12 nuclides at each site, log scale. SRS values are assigned-closure sums across 51 tanks (residual basis, decayed to 2032); Hanford values are BBI sums across 177 tanks (current basis, decayed predominantly to 2022-01-01). Nuclides are labeled with the Hanford BBI naming convention (mass number first)."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The mid-list is where the sites diverge. Samarium-151, a fission product with modest radiological hazard but a 90-year half-life, is third at SRS (1.46 MCi closure basis) and also third at Hanford (2.94 MCi), but its ratio to Sr-90 differs by a factor of 9.5 between the sites (Sm-151/Sr-90 of 0.85 at SRS versus 0.09 at Hanford), a signature of the sites' different decay ages and closure horizons. Zirconium-93 is reported by both inventories, at 497 Ci in SRS closure residuals and 3.5 kCi at Hanford, a ratio that reflects the residual-versus-current basis far more than generation. Plutonium-238 differs by two orders of magnitude: 0.20 MCi in SRS closure residuals versus 2.6 kCi in Hanford's BBI, consistent with SRS's dedicated Pu-238 production campaign for radioisotope heat sources, which has no Hanford analogue. Curium-244, americium-241, and plutonium-241 appear at both sites with SRS-to-Hanford ratios between 0.5 and 2. Technetium-99, the key long-lived mobile contaminant for both sites' performance assessments, is 25 kCi in Hanford's BBI and 239 Ci in SRS's closure-residual basis, a difference dominated by the residual-versus-current basis gap rather than by generation: SRS processes most Tc-99 into the Saltstone system."},
    {"t": "tbl2"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Nuclide-by-phase behavior is central to mission modeling and differs sharply. At Hanford, 91 percent of Sr-90 is in sludge phases while 54 percent of Cs-137 is in supernatant; at SRS the corresponding split is carried in the farm totals: most actinides and Sr-90 concentrate in sludge (106 MCi in 2.8 Mgal) while supernate carries 75 MCi dominated by Cs-137. The practical consequence for retrieval and treatment flowsheets is opposite: Hanford's big DSTs are cesium-rich liquids awaiting aluminosilicate or ion-exchange treatment, while SRS's big volumes are already staged for SWPF processing."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "2.4 Farm-level and per-tank distribution"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Figure 3 plots every tank at both sites by activity and capacity or volume. The Hanford distribution is heavy-tailed: the eight hottest tanks (241-AZ-101 at 14.7 MCi, 241-AZ-102 at 6.8 MCi, 241-AP-102 at 6.7 MCi, 241-AN-106 at 5.0 MCi, 241-AY-101 at 4.9 MCi, 241-A-106 at 3.0 MCi, 241-A-105 at 2.9 MCi, and 241-AN-104 at 2.9 MCi) hold 38 percent of site activity (46.97 of 122.53 MCi) in 4.5 percent of its tanks. All eight are A-farm or AX/AP/AZ DSTs or older A-farm SSTs, a concentration that directly motivates Hanford's sequencing priorities. SRS's per-tank assigned-closure distribution is flat by comparison: 51 tanks between 119 Ci (Tank 20, a closed Type IV) and 424 kCi (Tank 6), median 141 kCi. The SRS flatness is partly an artifact of the max-of-group assignment method (Section 3.4), which deliberately removes inter-tank variation for conservative PA purposes; for the three tanks with measured residuals the assigned values reproduce the actuals to within rounding (Tanks 18 and 19: 25.1 and 8.2 kCi on both bases)."},
    {"t": "fig", "file": "fig4_tank_scatter.png", "caption": "Figure 3. Every tank at both sites. Hanford points are current BBI activity against current waste volume; SRS points are assigned-closure activity against tank capacity, since SRS current per-tank volumes are not published. The axes are log-log."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Farm-level totals at Hanford are similarly concentrated: AZ farm alone (2 tanks) holds 21.6 MCi, 18 percent of the site total in 1.1 percent of its tanks; SX (15 tanks) 20.2 MCi, AN (7) 19.0, AP (8) 13.0, A (6) 9.2, AW (6) 7.9, S (12) 5.5, AY (2) 5.3, BY (12) 4.6, TX (18) 4.5, U (16) 3.8, SY (3) 2.6, AX (4) 2.0, and the B, BX, C, T, and TY farms together under 5 MCi. SRS's two farms are similar to each other on the closure basis: FTF 2.70 MCi across 22 tanks, HTF 3.11 MCi across 29 tanks, from the SA-reported totals. Farm closure sequencing at SRS (FTF first, then HTF) therefore does not follow a curie gradient but a tank-type and infrastructure logic."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "2.5 Chemical composition"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Figure 4 and Table 3 compare the chemical inventories. The bases differ more than for radionuclides: SRS chemical values are kg of element or anion on residual solids, while Hanford's are kg per tank across all phases including supernatant salts. The Hanford totals are therefore larger in part because they include dissolved salts. With that caveat, the contrast is stark: Hanford's non-water chemical inventory is 150,200 t, and 72 percent of it is Na, NO3, and NO2 combined (46,420 t Na, 50,066 t NO3, 11,904 t NO2), from the sodium hydroxide and nitrite additions that stabilized waste in alkaline brine. SRS's assigned residual-solids inventory is 299 t, dominated by process metals: 94 t Fe, 57 t Al, 18 t Mn, 13 t Ni, plus 19.3 t of mercury and 10.9 t of uranium."},
    {"t": "fig", "file": "fig5_chemicals.png", "text_skip": None, "caption": "Figure 4. Site-total chemical inventories for ten comparison elements, log scale. SRS values are assigned-closure residual solids; Hanford values are BBI totals across all phases. The bases differ (Section 3.4): Hanford includes supernatant salts, SRS is residual solids after bulk removal."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Three elements carry site-specific processing signatures. Mercury (19.3 t in SRS residuals) entered SRS waste from the mercury-catalyzed deborated-zeolite... the historical use of mercury as a catalyst in the HM process dissolver; SRS mercury management (amalgamation, steam stripping in the M Area, and later collection) kept most of it out of the tank farms, and DWPF now controls it in the melter offgas. Hanford's BBI reports 1.8 t of mercury site-wide, from the same bismuth-phosphate process chemistry that also loaded B-farm. Chromium tells the opposite story: Hanford holds 566 t, largely from sodium dichromate coolant added to radiatively-cooled single-shell tanks (Yakima-area river-water-cooled tanks did not receive it), which today complicates LAW vitrification and SST corrosion-legacy decisions; SRS's residual chromium is 0.75 t. Aluminum is the largest glass-former burden shared by both sites: 7,938 t at Hanford (from REDOX and PUREX cladding-dissolution chemistry) versus 57 t in SRS residuals, though SRS aluminum is better known through DWPF's ongoing sludge-batch control of Al2O3 loading (Section 2.6)."},
    {"t": "tbl3"},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "2.6 Measured current waste: SRS batch data"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The SRNL qualification reports give the only public per-analyte view of what is actually in SRS tanks today, at process-stream rather than tank level. Sludge Batch 11 (in Tank 51, a Type III/IIIA tank) was characterized in 2024 at 16,600 micrograms per gram Fe and 15,500 ug/g Al on a slurry basis (Table 4), with supernate at 110 g/L Na, 1.54 M free hydroxide, 89.9 g/L nitrate, and 25.8 g/L nitrite: the same nitrate-nitrite-hydroxide brine matrix that dominates Hanford's DSTs, at comparable concentration. Sludge Batch 10 radionuclides measured in Tank 40 in 2023 give the sludge-phase specific activity: 5,870 uCi/g dried solids Sr-90 (plus Y-90 in equilibrium), 403 uCi/g Cs-137, 101 uCi/g Pu-238, and 65.7 uCi/g Sm-151, with total sludge activity density near 3.7 Ci per gallon of slurry for Sr-90 alone."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Salt Batch 14, drawn from Tank 21H in 2025 for SWPF processing, measured 1.15e5 pCi/mL Tc-99, 5,380 pCi/mL Pu-241, 3,780 pCi/mL tritium, and full WAC-limit ratios: Tc-99 at 54 percent of its SWPF waste-acceptance-criteria limit, Pu-241 at 0.6 percent, and tritium at 0.7 percent of limit. These qualification measurements anchor the SRS current-waste picture that the closure tables cannot: they show a salt system deeply decontaminated in actinides (actinide concentrations four to five orders below the Cs-137 that drives SWPF decontamination), a Tc-99 stream sized for Saltstone, and a sludge system dominated by process metals at glass-former-relevant concentrations."},
    {"t": "tbl4"},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "2.7 Historical trend"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "SRS farm-total inventories have declined from 263 MCi (32.5 Mgal) at end of 2016 to 248 MCi (35.0 Mgal) at end of 2018 to 191 MCi (33.2 Mgal) at end of September 2025, a 27 percent activity reduction in nine years while volume held roughly flat (Figure 5). The decline tracks salt-waste processing ramp: supernate activity fell from 120 to 75 MCi between 2018 and 2025 and saltcake from 12 to 10 MCi, while sludge activity fell modestly from 116 to 106 MCi as DWPF consumes sludge batches. Hanford's BBI snapshots are per-tank effective dates rather than a site-wide series, so no comparable trend series can be drawn from the BBI alone; retrieval to date has been limited to a small number of tanks and the site's remaining inventory is effectively static relative to SRS's."},
    {"t": "fig", "file": "fig1_srs_decline.png", "caption": "Figure 5. SRS tank-farm total activity by phase, December 2016 to September 2025, from the WCS reports in the Liquid Waste System Plan series. The 2016 salt split was not published with a supernate/saltcake breakdown; it is shown as supernate."},
]
